WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:05.940
The original version of Pixar's Luca was going
to be very different, from a huge monster

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:10.460
movie ending, to a quest for magical tokens,
and a missing John Ratzenberger cameo.

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:15.090
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and in
this video I'm revealing the alternate ending

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:18.919
of Luca and some of the scenes and storylines
you never got to see!

00:00:18.919 --> 00:00:21.609
Spoilers ahead, of course, so take care.

00:00:21.609 --> 00:00:26.150
Before Pixar's filmmakers landed on the beautiful
bittersweet ending for Luca we see in the

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:31.499
final film, the movie's finale was originally
going to be more of a giant monster battle.

00:00:31.499 --> 00:00:36.570
This particular story leaned much more heavily
than the final film into the division between

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:41.969
humans and seas monsters, setting them up
as two diametrically opposed factions or worlds,

00:00:41.969 --> 00:00:46.519
which director Enrico Casarosa compared in
an interview with SlashFilm to the divide

00:00:46.519 --> 00:00:49.520
between the Capulets and Montagues in Romeo
&amp; Juliet.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:54.690
Although in the final film the townspeople
of Portorosso are still fearful of sea monsters

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:59.170
with a reward being set up after a recent
sighting of one, it sounds like in the earlier

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:04.030
version this confrontation between the two
worlds was going to be even more intense,

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:08.510
with the original plan being for Alberto to
transform into a kraken, an enormous mythical

00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:13.260
sea monster that looks like a humungous octopus
or squid, with Luca then standing up to defend

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:17.750
and protect his friend.
However, as Casarosa told Cinema Blend a conversation

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:22.660
he had with his own real-life best friend
ended up inspiring him to completely retool

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:26.900
the film's finale.
That movie-changing chat was all about their

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:31.770
own friendship and how good friends help each
other grow but eventually that often takes

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:35.870
them down very different paths in life, leading
to bittersweet goodbyes.

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:40.850
For Casarosa, that's something he's experienced
several times in his own life, having left

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:45.890
his home in Italy for America to pursue his
dreams of animation and filmmaking.

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:51.000
And when he and his team put those ideas together,
storyboarded them, and added composer Dan

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.630
Romer's score, they were hit by just how emotional
it was.

00:01:54.630 --> 00:02:00.360
So, the film dropped its colossal kraken and,
as Casarosa told SlashFilm, focused instead

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:06.110
on "a kid's world and stakes and goals, with
this kind of slightly strange and silly idea:

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:10.640
"We're gonna ride into the sunset with a Vespa!"
Curiously enough, in the final film there

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:15.420
is actually an easter egg to a giant sea monster
movie though, via the poster for the Disney

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:20.220
adaption of Jules Vernes' "20,000 Leagues
Under The Sea", which features an attack by

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:24.140
a giant squid.
And there's also an illustration of a giant

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:29.100
kraken-like creature wrapping its tentacles
around a boat on one of the fishermen's maps.

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:33.570
Over the years, Krakens have popped up in
quite a few well-known movies including The

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:39.250
Clash of The Titans, the Pirates of the Caribbean
franchise, and Hotel Transylvania 3: Summer

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:41.370
Vacation,
which is funny as the protagonists of both

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:46.120
that movie and Luca are monsters and both
films are set during the summer holidays!

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:50.680
In fact, the kraken plays a key part in the
ending of the third Hotel Transylvania movie,

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:55.050
and given that film came out in 2018 while
Luca was still in development at Pixar, I

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:59.630
wonder if it might have also influenced the
decision to change Luca’s finale.

00:02:59.630 --> 00:03:04.210
When Casarosa first came up with the idea
for the movie, Luca and Alberto were not the

00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:06.810
only young sea monsters who featured in the
story;

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:10.730
there was actually a group of them, giving
the film what the director has called a Stand

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:14.740
By Me vibe, referring to the 1980s coming-of-age
classic.

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:19.130
In fact, one of those additional sea monster
friends was called Ciccio, a name you'll probably

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:23.980
recognise from the final film as his original
role was completely rewritten to make him

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:26.930
one of bully boy Ercole's human side-kicks
instead.

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:32.099
And there's a lot of lovely concept art by
Chris Sasaki which shows the original trio

00:03:32.099 --> 00:03:36.011
of Luca, Alberto and Ciccio getting up to
various hijinks in both their sea monster

00:03:36.011 --> 00:03:40.010
and human forms.
Judging by Ciccio's build in the final film,

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:43.430
it looks like this character here was the
sea monster version of him.

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:48.130
It's interesting that sea monster Ciccio had
crab claws for arms and carried around a shell

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:52.300
on his back, like a hermit crab.
And a remnant of this original idea made its

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:56.250
way into Luca's surname "Paguro" which means
hermit crab in Italian.

00:03:56.250 --> 00:04:01.680
As for Giulia, it seems a human girl was part
of the group of male sea monster friends even

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:05.360
in early versions of the story;
however, the filmmakers did experiment with

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:10.380
when exactly to reveal the truth to her that
her new pals were not actually human.

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:14.310
You can see in the Art of Luca book that at
one point she learned about them earlier as

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:16.959
she goes swimming with them in their sea monster
form.

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:21.430
And in another scene she's with all three
original sea creature friends and they have

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:24.870
their tails showing as they sit down for dinner
with Massimo.

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:29.060
In the end, Casarosa and his team decided
that prolonging that reveal till later in

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:34.610
the film "added more tension and drama", as
producer Andrea Warren told website io9.

00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:39.210
In what would have been another major change
to the story, originally the kids went on

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:44.479
a much bigger quest that involved magical
tokens and their intention was to become completely

00:04:44.479 --> 00:04:47.360
human.
The final film certainly has shades of The

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:51.860
Little Mermaid with Alberto collecting items
from the human world and Luca being forbidden

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:56.219
from going to the surface, but it sounds like
this original plot point would have made the

00:04:56.219 --> 00:04:59.409
film feel even more like Pixar's The Little
Merman.

00:04:59.409 --> 00:05:04.729
Likewise, Luca's original storyline where
the young sea monsters set out to become fully-fledged

00:05:04.729 --> 00:05:09.349
human boys also has a ring about it of the
Italian children's novel Pinocchio, which

00:05:09.349 --> 00:05:13.610
was adapted into a movie by Disney.
And there are several easter eggs to the wooden

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:18.199
marionette who desperately wanted to become
a real human boy in the finished Pixar film.

00:05:18.199 --> 00:05:23.240
However, Casarosa says that storyline got
too complicated and he preferred a "more lyrical

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:26.639
take on things".
So, he decided to strip everything back to

00:05:26.639 --> 00:05:30.940
what he realised should be the heart of the
film: the friendship between Luca and Alberto,

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:34.300
and he made those two children the centre
of the story.

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:39.350
Further work was still needed on the characters
because, as Luca's a bit introverted and very

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:43.479
quiet, Casarosa found that in some earlier
versions of the script, it was difficult for

00:05:43.479 --> 00:05:47.939
the audience to understand him.
So, subconsciously inspired by his love of

00:05:47.939 --> 00:05:53.150
Fellini's film 8½ and its dream sequences,
for the final film Casarosa decided to take

00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:57.900
us literally inside Luca's head by showing
us his daydreams.

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:01.689
Another thing that evolved during development
was where the sea monsters lived.

00:06:01.689 --> 00:06:06.279
Originally, they were "hiding in plain sight"
in their own town on an island, as you can

00:06:06.279 --> 00:06:09.319
see in this concept art here from the Art
of Luca book.

00:06:09.319 --> 00:06:14.069
And another piece of concept art shows how
the monster island was shaped like an octopus.

00:06:14.069 --> 00:06:19.590
However, Casarosa and his team decided they
only needed one town, so they "just let the

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:23.669
sea monsters be well-hidden" instead.
They did still keep the idea of an island

00:06:23.669 --> 00:06:27.320
though, but just as the location of Alberto's
tower, and there may be another trace of the

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:31.380
original concept in the way that one of the
fishermen is afraid to go there.

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:35.080
"Do we really need to fish near the island?"
"You worry too much."

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:37.740
"I don't know.
What if the old stories are true?"

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:41.400
Some other interesting alternatives that were
considered for the monster world are these

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:45.650
illustrations of an underwater town that is
similar in look to the human town and which

00:06:45.650 --> 00:06:49.809
appears to be situated almost directly underneath
Portorosso.

00:06:49.809 --> 00:06:53.559
Again there appears to be a remnant of this
original idea on the back of the playing card

00:06:53.559 --> 00:06:57.779
that Luca finds which has the picture of a
town in the top half which is reflected in

00:06:57.779 --> 00:07:00.819
the lower half.
There was also an alternative to Alberto's

00:07:00.819 --> 00:07:04.930
tower for his collection of human objects
in the form of a secret hideout inside an

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:09.770
upside-down semi-submerged shipwreck.
The entrance was via the water with a dry

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:13.409
area in the hull above where Alberto stored
his whosits and whatsits.

00:07:13.409 --> 00:07:17.910
And it looks like the boat was located in
a cave, again giving it a bit of a Little

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:18.999
Mermaid vibe.

00:07:18.999 --> 00:07:23.830
A regular Pixar feature that didn't make it
into the final movie is a cameo by John Ratzenberger.

00:07:23.830 --> 00:07:29.500
Before Soul, the Cheers actor had voiced at
least one character in every Pixar film.

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:34.419
In Soul, Ratzenberger didn't voice any character;
however, the movie's co-director Kemp Powers

00:07:34.419 --> 00:07:38.470
said that a tribute to him was made via a
non-speaking character who appears briefly

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:42.649
in the subway.
As for Luca, Casarosa confirmed that Ratzenberger

00:07:42.649 --> 00:07:46.529
is not in the film and said on Twitter that
he hoped to start a new tradition of having

00:07:46.529 --> 00:07:51.149
The Good Dinosaur director Peter Sohn voice
a character in each new Pixar movie.

00:07:51.149 --> 00:07:54.699
In Luca, Sohn is the voice of Ciccio.
"Well, my head kinda hurts..."

00:07:54.699 --> 00:07:57.409
"Not you, Ciccio!"
I'm curious as to whether Ratzenberger has

00:07:57.409 --> 00:08:01.849
now retired from Pixar films for good or if
his absence from Soul and Luca is related

00:08:01.849 --> 00:08:05.589
to the pandemic as both films were completed
during that time.

00:08:05.589 --> 00:08:10.309
Intriguingly, I found an old man sea monster
character in some concept art that I wonder

00:08:10.309 --> 00:08:12.059
if he might have played.

